The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
clk_unregister(plat_data->pclk);
clk_unregister(plat_data->hclk);
platform_device_unregister(plat_dev);
</text>
Reason:
The clocks are unregistered before the platform device is unregistered. During `platform_device_unregister()`, the runtime resume callback (`macb_runtime_resume`) may be invoked, which calls `clk_prepare()` and `clk_enable()` on the already-freed clock objects, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
struct clk *pclk = plat_data->pclk;
struct clk *hclk = plat_data->hclk;
platform_device_unregister(plat_dev);
clk_unregister(pclk);
clk_unregister(hclk);
</text>
Reason:
The clock pointers are saved locally before unregistering the platform device. Then `platform_device_unregister()` is called first, ensuring any runtime resume calls during device removal can still safely access the clocks. After the platform device is fully removed, the clocks are unregistered, avoiding the use-after-free.

